lesson art-symmetry-with-the-mirror v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=2 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Symmetry with a mirror"
  goal "Finish a picture across a mirror line, check a picture for symmetry, and paint a butterfly with the mirror."

  look {
    scene 25x11 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        stamps print motif=butterfly show="print" colour=pink flip=false made="print"
        paintsheet half w=12 h=8 paper="squared" guide="mirror" outline="butterfly" pots=[]
      }
    }
    say """
      A butterfly is the same on both sides of its body. The line down the middle is a mirror line:
      whatever is on one side is on the other side too, turned round. A mirror, or a fold in the
      paper, can make the second side for you.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.finish-the-mirror count=2 seed=4
    level easy {
      worked art.finish-the-mirror-big s=6 k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice art.finish-the-mirror-big count=2 seed=2
    practice art.whole-butterfly count=2 seed=5
    practice art.mirror-faces count=2 hard-count=1 seed=1
    level hard {
      practice art.mirror-halves count=1 seed=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.mirror-halves n=12 easy-n=6 hard-n=16
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.paint-a-butterfly
  }
  remember {
    say "A mirror turns a picture round, so left becomes right, and each square has a twin."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The machine marks which half finishes the mirror and which whole butterfly is the same on both
    sides. It builds the three halves from the same pattern every time, one of them the half copied
    across without turning, which is the mistake most children make first, and one with a single
    square changed, which only careful checking finds. On screen the mirror button paints the second
    wing as the child paints the first. On paper, paint one side thickly, fold along the line, press,
    and open it.

    The two-star question runs the mirror backwards: from the number of squares in the finished
    butterfly to the number the child paints. The butterfly is the three-star question, because the
    child has to plan three shapes a wing and mix a colour before the mirror does its half.

    Respond by looking at the two sides together: "The spot near the top came out on both wings, the
    same distance from the body." Ask what the fold did to a line that crossed the middle.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the butterflies have one to three shapes on each wing, a big one with six shapes is finished once in the teacher's pen, the faces are as written, the two-star wants six squares in all, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the butterflies have four to six shapes on each wing, one face makes way for a second count of squares with the mirror on, the two-star wants sixteen squares in all, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
